-- title:
--   Kechris Thms 17.23–17.24 — $P(X)$ is standard Borel when $X$ is standard Borel
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be a standard Borel space. Then the space $P(X)$ of probability measures on $X$, equipped with the $\sigma$-algebra generated by the maps $\mu\mapsto\mu(A)$ for measurable $A\subseteq X$ (the Giry $\sigma$-algebra), is again a standard Borel space.
--
--   This follows from Kechris, Theorems 17.23 and 17.24: choosing a Polish topology on $X$ generating its Borel sets, $P(X)$ with the weak topology is Polish (17.23) and its Borel $\sigma$-algebra is generated by the maps $\mu\mapsto\mu(A)$ (17.24).
-- source:
--   A. S. Kechris, Classical Descriptive Set Theory (GTM 156, Springer 1995), §17.E, Theorems 17.23 and 17.24 (see also the remark following 17.24 that P(X) is standard Borel for standard Borel X)

import Mathlib

open MeasureTheory

/-- Kechris, Classical Descriptive Set Theory, Theorems 17.23–17.24: if `X` is a standard Borel
space, so is `P(X)`, the space of probability measures on `X` with the σ-algebra generated by
the maps `μ ↦ μ(A)`, `A` measurable (the Giry σ-algebra). -/
theorem ProbabilityMeasureSpace.standardBorelSpace {X : Type*} [MeasurableSpace X]
    [StandardBorelSpace X] :
    StandardBorelSpace (ProbabilityMeasure X) := by sorry
